@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.30}Exercise 4.30:} Cy D. Fect, a reformed C
programmer, is worried that some side effects may never take place in a lazy
evaluator that doesn't force the expressions in a sequence.  Since the value
of the expression @code{e1} in a sequence @code{e1; e2} is not used (the
expression is there only for its effect, such as assigning to a reference or
printing), there can be no subsequent use of this value (e.g., as an argument
to a primitive procedure) that will cause it to be forced.  Cy thus thinks
that when evaluating sequences, we must force all expressions in the sequence
except the final one.  The @code{Sequence} clause of @code{Sec_4_2} does just
that:

@example ocaml
| Ast.Sequence (first, second) ->
  let* first = self env first in
  let* _ = actual_value self counters first in
  self env second
@end example

@noindent
Consider the alternative clause that leaves out the call of
@code{actual_value}, as the lazy evaluator of the original book did.

@enumerate a

@item
Ben Bitdiddle thinks the alternative would be good enough.  He shows Cy the
