@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.31}Exercise 4.31:} The approach taken in this
section is somewhat unpleasant, because it makes an incompatible change to
the language.  It might be nicer to implement lazy evaluation as an
@newterm{upward-compatible extension}, that is, so that ordinary programs will
work as before.  We can do this by extending the syntax of procedure
declarations to let the user control whether or not arguments are to be
delayed.  While we're at it, we may as well also give the user the choice
between delaying with and without memoization.  For example, the declaration

@example
let f a (b lazy) c (d lazy_memo) = @r{…}
@end example

@noindent
would define @code{f} to be a procedure of four arguments, where the first and
third arguments are evaluated when the procedure is called, the second argument
is delayed, and the fourth argument is both delayed and memoized.  Thus,
ordinary procedure declarations will produce the same behavior as ordinary
OCaml, while adding the @code{lazy_memo} declaration to each parameter of
every compound procedure will produce the behavior of the lazy evaluator
defined in this section.  Design and implement the changes required to produce
such an extension.  @code{Check} admits only OCaml syntax, so you will have to
carry the parameter declarations as data beside the abstract syntax of the
procedure.  You must also arrange for @code{eval} or @code{apply} to determine
when arguments are to be delayed, and to force or delay arguments accordingly,
and you must arrange for forcing to memoize or not, as appropriate.
@end quotation

@node	4.2.3, 4.3, 4.1, 4.2
@subsection Streams as Lazy Lists

In @ref{3.5.1}, we showed how to implement streams as delayed lists.
We used a delayed tail, which allowed us to construct a ``promise'' to compute
the rest of a stream, without actually fulfilling that promise until later.
We could use this general technique of introducing special forms whenever we
need more control over the evaluation process, but this is awkward.  For one
thing, a special form is not a first-class object like a procedure, so we
cannot use it together with higher-order procedures.@footnote{This is
precisely the issue with the @code{unless} procedure, as in
@ref{Exercise 4.26}.}  Additionally, we were forced to create streams as a new
kind of data object similar but not identical to lists, and this required us
to reimplement many ordinary list operations (@code{map}, @code{append}, and so
on) for use with streams.

With lazy evaluation, streams and lists can be identical, so there is no need
for special forms or for separate list and stream operations.  All we need to
do is to arrange matters so that @code{cons} is non-strict.  One way to
accomplish this is to extend the lazy evaluator to allow for non-strict
primitives, and to implement @code{::} as one of these.  An easier way is to
recall (@ref{2.1.3}) that there is no fundamental need to use the built-in
constructor directly at all.  Instead, we can make the construction of a pair
the body of a compound procedure, which is non-strict in its
arguments:@footnote{This is close to the procedural representation described
in @ref{Exercise 2.4}, which a typed language cannot use for lists of any
length, because the type of such a list would contain itself.  The compound
procedure keeps OCaml's list type and moves only the construction behind a
procedure call.  Notice that we can install this definition in the lazy
evaluator simply by declaring it in the program.  (Also see
@ref{Exercise 4.33} and @ref{Exercise 4.34}.)}

@example ocaml
let cons x y = x :: y
@end example

@noindent
When @code{cons} is applied, both operands become thunks, and the body builds
a list cell that holds the two thunks.  A @code{match} forces only the parts
its pattern tests.  In terms of this basic operation, the standard definitions
of the list operations will work with infinite lists (streams) as well as
finite ones, and the stream operations can be implemented as list operations.
Here are some examples:

@example ocaml
let rec list_ref items n =
  match items with
  | x :: rest -> if n = 0 then x else list_ref rest (n - 1)
  | [] -> 0

let rec map proc items =
  match items with
  | x :: rest -> cons (proc x) (map proc rest)
  | [] -> []

let scale_list items factor = map (fun x -> x * factor) items

let rec add_lists list1 list2 =
  match list1, list2 with
  | [], _ -> list2
  | _, [] -> list1
  | x :: xs, y :: ys -> cons (x + y) (add_lists xs ys)

let rec ones = 1 :: ones

let integers =
  let knot = ref [] in
  let result = cons 1 (add_lists ones !knot) in
  knot := result;
  result

let () = print_int (list_ref integers 17)
@end example

@noindent
Under the lazy experiment, the program prints @code{18}.  OCaml's @code{let rec} admits the cyclic list
@code{ones}, whose recursive occurrence lies beneath a constructor, but not a
recursive value defined by an application.  The definition of
@code{integers} therefore ties the knot through a reference.  Nothing here
delays anything explicitly: the operand @code{!knot} is read only when the
tail of @code{integers} is forced, after the assignment.

@noindent
Note that these lazy lists are even lazier than the streams of @ref{Chapter 3}:
The head of the list, as well as the tail, is
delayed.@footnote{This permits us to create delayed versions of more general
kinds of list structures, not just sequences.  @ref{Hughes 1990} discusses some
applications of ``lazy trees.''}  In fact, even taking a lazy list apart need
not force the value of a list element.  The value will be forced only when it
is really needed, e.g., for use as the argument of a primitive, or to be
printed.

Lazy pairs also help with the problem that arose with streams in
@ref{3.5.4}, where we found that formulating stream models of systems with
loops may require us to sprinkle our programs with explicit @code{delay}
operations, beyond the ones that build each stream.  With lazy evaluation,
all arguments to procedures are delayed uniformly.  For instance, we can
implement procedures to integrate lists and solve differential equations as we
originally intended in @ref{3.5.4}, where @code{map}, @code{scale_list}, and
@code{add_lists} are the versions above over @code{float}:

@example ocaml
let integral integrand initial_value dt =
  let int = ref [] in
  let result =
    cons initial_value
      (add_lists (scale_list integrand dt) !int)
  in
  int := result;
  result

let solve f y0 dt =
  let y = ref [] in
  let result = integral (map f !y) y0 dt in
  y := result;
  result

let () =
  print_endline
    (string_of_float
       (list_ref (solve (fun x -> x) 1.0 0.001) 1000))
@end example

@noindent
Under the lazy experiment, the program prints @code{2.71692393224}.

